course: dictionary
lesson: 11082
title: Old word, new surprise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old, and you will meet them mostly in old stories and poems. The verb wend is one of these. It means to travel slowly, often along a path that bends and winds. Picture a walker taking the long way round the hill: that walker is wending their way.
explain: Look at the context clue «wend your way». The word your tells you that wend is something a person does, and the gentle feel of the phrase tells you the movement is slow. Try the swap test: "She rushed her way home" does not fit the calm picture, so wend must mean something slower.
explain: Wend has four forms: wend, wends, wended and wending. Wended is the past, and wend is a regular verb, so you add -ed to make it. Wend is hardly ever used in everyday speech, so when you meet it, notice the clues around it.
fact: Wend means to travel slowly, often following a winding path.
fact: Wend is used almost only in the phrase «wend your way», which gives a story an old-fashioned, gentle feel.
fact: The forms of wend are wend, wends, wended and wending.
fact: «Went» was once the past of the old verb «wend», meaning to go, and English borrowed it for go hundreds of years ago.
fact: When a verb's forms come from different words, it is called suppletion, and «be» (am, is, was, were) is built the same patchwork way.
exercise: «She wended her way along the winding river path.» What does wended mean? | *Swam across the river | Travelled slowly along a winding path | Stopped and sat down to rest | Ran fast to catch a bus
exercise: «The old man wended his way through the village.» Find and copy the verb in this sentence. | through | *wended | old | village
exercise: Which form is the past of wend in regular use? | wending | *wended | wends | wend
test: «The lane wends gently between the hedges.» What does wends mean here? | Stops suddenly | Shouts loudly | *Travels slowly along a winding path | Falls down
test: Which phrase is wend mostly used in, giving a story an old-fashioned feel? | *wend your way | get home quickly | shout hello | run and jump
exam: Which statement about wend is correct? | Wended is the present form of wend | Wend means to travel quickly and straight to the point | Wend is a common word in everyday texting | *Wend means to travel slowly, often along a winding path
